So strap in for a particularly stabby one today,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.640
and quite honestly over the next couple weeks,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.540
because I'm starting in on what's turning out to be

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.800
one of the most monumental feats of my entire Film Theory career.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.080
I am dissecting every major Disney movie.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.320
That means every Disney animated film that's been in theaters since 1939,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.160
to determine once and for all which is the deadliest of all the Disney movies.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.600
And, if I can swing it, what is the overall total kill count for the entire Disney canon?

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.260
And whoo-boy! It is so much harder than it looks.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.539
In case you haven't noticed over the years,

00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:43.620
Disney movies, for all their cute talking animals, can get surprisingly dark,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.800
and the characters are pretty darn good at killing one another,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.960
and in some really creative ways, too.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.240
I mean Clayton in "Tarzan" hanging himself with a vine,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.920
"Oliver and Company"s electrocution by subway track,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.660
Some of this stuff is better than the slasher movies that you see in Dead Meat's "Killcount" series.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:04.319
I mean sure, some Disney movies only managed light emotional scarring:

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.100
"Mother!"

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.760
"Mother, where are you?!"

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.200
But when you start dealing with dragons, snow monsters and very sharp sewing equipment,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.720
[spooky sound]

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.020
You start running into some serious collateral damage in a hurry,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.580
but just how much are we talking about?

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.660
Well, it is way more than you'd think.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:31.440
Especially considering that this lineup is all G or PG rated, and intended for kids.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.740
But, looking across all of Disney's history, across its sixty Disney animation studio theatrical releases,

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.300
I'm aiming to find out where the mouse is not just killing it at the box office,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.920
but also literally killing everyone you see on screen.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.800
And if you think this is just a matter of a little counting here and there, you are sorely mistaken.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.400
There is so much more here than meets the eye,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.780
as evidenced by the fact that I thought this was just gonna be one straightforward episode

00:03:55.780 --> 00:04:00.760
and has now blown up into a three-episode mini saga through the depths of the Disney catalogue,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.600
covering everything from the ecology of the Pacific Islands,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.100
to the biology of the African Serengeti,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.580
to the ancient history of the Grecian Isles.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.519
Oh, yeah, we are going thorough with this one!

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:13.840
I'm telling you now; by the end of this little miniseries,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.440
you are gonna be struggling to crawl out from under the heaps of animated bodies piled up on top of you!

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.260
So, what is the deadliest Disney movie?

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.050
Place your bets now, and see if you're right at the end!

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.635
I don't know, you can make a little bet about it with your family,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.920
or whoever you feel comfortable talking about this topic with.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.922
Hey Mom and Dad, what do you think the deadliest Disney movie is?

00:04:31.922 --> 00:04:34.560
Bet you five bucks it's "Meet the Robinsons"!

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:39.600
Don't do that, that would be a stupid, stupid bet. Spoiler alert: it is not "Meet the Robinsons".

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.480
Like I said, approaching the concept of the most deadly Disney movie is deceptively complicated.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.340
Take for instance; this rabbit,

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.240
this rabbit,

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.220
and this rabbit.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.749
They're all the same animal across three different movies,

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:54.280
but would you count them all in a death total if they died?

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.420
One is the white rabbit from Alice in Wonderland, one is Thumper from Bambi,

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:03.180
and one is just... guess like one of Snow White's supportive woodland friends.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.480
They're all alive, they're all cute bunnies, but for the purposes of this theory, they don't all count.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.940
And this is where we have to lay ourselves some ground rules.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.630
Should we count the rabbit who carries a pocket watch, because he's wearing people clothes?

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.680
What about the one who defends Bambi in his time of crisis?

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.867
For this theory, I drew the line around sentience versus non sentience of the characters deaths.

00:05:22.867 --> 00:05:29.100
One thing that's really obvious in Disney movies is that different species may or may not be sentient in any given movie.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:35.360
Usually humans or at least humanoids take the spotlight, but in movies like The Lion King, animals are the focus.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.770
They are clearly sentient, and most importantly for our purposes, they can speak.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:46.200
So let's just set the rule: If animals can speak or if they act as intentionally driven characters like Maleficent's pet raven, Diablo,

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.160
then they count in our overall death total.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.080
We also can't extrapolate beyond actions taken in the movie that we're watching.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:59.620
Did the colony established by John Smith eventually go on to kill thousands of natives in the 1995 "Pocahontas"?

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.780
Yeah, absolutely, but that doesn't happen during the runtime of the movie,

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.540
because we can't have that punky white guy lose, right?

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:11.880
So he gets a pass- at least in this context, history would beg to differ.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.960
And finally, we can only count kills that are actually caused by the characters in the movie.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:22.560
For example, in "Hercules", we can't count the actions taken off screen by Hades in his normal role as god of death,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.577
since he's just presiding over the people who are dying as usual.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:30.320
Instead, we can only count the actions he takes directly against Hercules and the other gods.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.900
Also guys, no sequels.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.040
There is nothing less satisfying than coming out at the end of one of these really long analyses,

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.860
only to find out that "Cinderella 3: A Twist In Time" actually kills the most people,

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.440
because it creates this time paradox that threatens to destroy Cinderella, and her entire kingdom.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.460
That takes "Ralph Breaks the Internet" and "Rescuers Down Under" off the table.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.240
Yeah, just take my word for it guys.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.040
We're all dodging a bullet by sticking only to the original in theater releases.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.022
We're already covering fifty-eight other movies.

00:06:58.022 --> 00:06:59.769
These are long episodes!

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:06.340
So maybe if you like this concept, we can do another tournament of the sequels, or Pixar movies or whatever, just later.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:08.800
I need a Disney break after this one.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.240
So with our handy-dandy rulebook established, we're ready to face death head on!

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.050
Or, at least in our Disney movies.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:18.880
Our first task is to carve up our fifty-eight contender movies into more manageable categories,

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.300
to see who has a fighting chance, or should I say a dying chance of claiming the ultimate title:

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.240
Disney's deadliest movie.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.520
If you look across the spectrum, from 1939 to today,

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.080
You'll see that these movies fall into a few pretty distinct categories when it comes to death.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.820
You have no death movies,

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.540
single or double death movies,

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:38.919
low kill count movies,

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:41.300
and then you have the really heavy hitters.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:45.038
There aren't too many mid-level death movies in Disney's canon, surprisingly.

00:07:45.039 --> 00:07:49.509
You're either all-in on the murder, or you're not and you're questionably family-friendly.

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:52.340
Our job is to figure out who's in that last category,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.900
and then suss out who's the biggest offender from the last eighty years of filmography?

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:01.120
Starting at the very beginning, there is a whole slew of movies in the Disney canon,

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.000
where the kill count is either zero, or just a really low number.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:09.980
Movies like "Dumbo", "The Jungle Book", "Sword in the Stone" and "101 Dalmatians" are all no kill movies,

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.920
[cheering noise]

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:16.190
as are "Robin Hood" and the feature films of "Winnie the Pooh".

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:23.020
In our no-kill films, the antagonist is usually thwarted in some comedic way, and then just kind of gives up their evil plot.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:24.660
Cruella Deville from 101 Dalmatians?

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.690
Car wreck, and just gives up!

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:27.920
Also while we're talking about it,

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.100
I'd be remiss if I didn't play this gem of a line from "101 Dalmatians".

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.640
"Crazy woman driver!"

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.660
It's great. Old-school Disney was so inappropriate!

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:39.080
Shere Khan from "The Jungle Book":

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.620
Also, fire on tail, just gives up!

00:08:41.622 --> 00:08:46.240
Or, quite frankly, there's just not enough movie there to have time to kill anyone off!

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.200
Man, a lot of these movies are a lot shorter than I remember.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.680
What's that one supposed to mean you ask?

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:58.880
Well, I'm talking about the Disney movies you probably haven't seen or even heard about from Disney's forgotten decade: the 1940s

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.735
The reason it's the Forgotten decade is probably because during the 1940s,

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:07.800
a large portion of Disney's writing and animation staff were drafted into World War II,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.180
sometimes leaving their stories half done or half animated,

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:13.580
and making it necessary for whoever has left in the studio

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.300
to cobble together a feature film from whatever was left.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.420
The movies that came out during this time kind of had plots,

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:24.220
they had little to no dialogue and are sometimes even just repurposed pieces of animation

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:28.370
that were left over from other movies while everyone else was out supporting the war effort.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:34.060
This is why so many of these no-kill movies are musical reviews featuring questionable live-action segments

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.900
of Donald Duck sexually harassing women, and totally uncomfortable race relations.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:44.149
It's interesting to note here too that the drafted Disney animators weren't trading in their pens for guns.

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:49.219
They weren't out on the front lines fighting, they were actually still wielding their animator pencils.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:53.160
They were still developing films using the exact same skills they used at Disney,

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.680
only now they were drafted into making propaganda films for the war effort.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.560
In fact, if you want a really fascinating watch, check out "Victory Through Air Power "

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.940
an official Disney release that outlines the history of air combat which in and of itself is fascinating,

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.630
but also was one of their first major propaganda films meant for public consumption.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:15.589
I just didn't include it in the overall kill count since, you know, it's World War II.

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:18.669
There were a lot of deaths but they were historically accurate deaths.

00:10:18.669 --> 00:10:22.920
It's just a fun fact that I came across while I was researching for this episode, and I thought you might enjoy,

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:28.600
but it also explains why you don't see a lot of full character arcs, or deaths during the films from this time period.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.840
It also makes you look at propaganda films and Disney movies just a little bit differently.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.960
Probably merits a theory for another day.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.860
Regardless, for as short, boring, and culturally inappropriate as a lot of these might be,

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:43.360
for this theory I'm digging up all the skeletons of the past,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:48.440
and need to know whether anyone dies in Disney features, like the "Three Caballeros" and "Fun and Fancy-Free",

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:53.427
and holy shattered childhood, some of these movies are shockingly brutal.

00:10:53.427 --> 00:10:58.840
While most of them do rack up zero kills, you have yourself some horrific surprises,

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.160
like "Make Mine Music",

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.460
where a whale whose only goal in life is to sing at the opera,

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.820
gets himself harpooned to death in a mistake,

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:09.827
and takes four sailors down in the process.

00:11:09.827 --> 00:11:12.020
Also a wolf dies so that one has a total of six.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:18.220
Or you have "Melody Time", where we see the story of Little Toot, who honestly should be renamed big body-count ,

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:22.920
after his pranks decimate a giant tanker and an entire city block.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.780
That's a body count in the double, if not triple digits!

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:29.400
Next, there's also a slew of movies where only the head villain gets the axe,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:33.460
or just the head villain and like, one or two other people he kills during his first scene of the movie,

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.450
just to show how evil of a villain he is.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.559
In these cases, they've already shown themselves to be a hundred percent irredeemable,

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.560
so you don't have to feel badly about their gruesome grotesque murder.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.510
"Snow White"? Only the Evil Queen dies.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:46.860
"Sleeping Beauty"? Maleficent and her underling raven Diablo.

